#!/bin/zsh -l
# Liveness channel for the careerforge nightly backup (M14-04 residual).
#
# The 02:00 job's only failure channel was an unread log, and six silent
# failures proved that is no channel at all. This check runs at 09:00 and
# alerts LOUDLY unless a fresh backup artifact exists in the backup directory.
# "Cannot verify" - card absent, directory missing, permission denied - is an
# ALERT, never a skip: silence was the original defect.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO LEGS, CHECKED SEPARATELY (M16-10)
#
# The backup fans out: db-backup writes to a directory on the SD64 card, and
# kura archives that directory into an age-encrypted container and ships it to
# the intel MBP. This check covers BOTH and never lets one hide the other:
#
#   usage: careerforge-backup-liveness [kura-project-name ...]
#
# Named projects are checked on intel; with none named the intel leg is
# reported as NOT CHECKED in the log rather than passed over in silence. The
# project names arrive as ARGUMENTS, not environment, so the agent's plist
# shows at a glance which legs it watches.
#
# The intel leg is EVALUATED FIRST and REPORTED LAST, and its one-line summary
# is appended to every alert this script raises. That ordering is the whole
# point: a broken card leg would otherwise exit before intel was ever looked
# at, and an operator would fix the card believing the second copy was fine.
# The SD64 states are reported first among the alerts because that leg is the
# one kura ships FROM - it is the cause, not the symptom - but the intel state
# rides along on the same line either way.
#
# A REPLICATION LEG NOBODY CHECKS IS NOT A BACKUP LEG, and this file's own
# history is the argument: an earlier version checked only the database dump
# while the profile tar "could have stopped being produced entirely and this
# check would have reported OK forever".
#
# Named without an extension so launchd and the macOS "Login Items &
# Extensions" UI show "careerforge-backup-liveness" rather than a bare "zsh",
# matching its sibling careerforge-backup. The login shell (-l) sources the
# user profile so node is on PATH under launchd's minimal environment.
#
# Config comes from the repo's .env (read, never sourced; values never echoed).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE LISTING GOES THROUGH node (rationale REWRITTEN 2026-09-02, M16-10)
#
# READ THIS BEFORE "SIMPLIFYING" THE LISTING BACK TO `ls`. The reason the node
# delegation was introduced is gone; the reasons to keep it are not, and they
# are different reasons. Leaving the old ones in place would invite exactly the
# change this paragraph exists to prevent.
#
# THE ORIGINAL REASON, NOW HISTORICAL. This check false-alarmed every morning
# on 2026-08-13 and 2026-08-14, logging "NO backup artifacts found in the
# backup dir at all" while six healthy dumps sat in that directory. The
# measured cause: under a launchd agent, macOS TCC permitted stat() but denied
# readdir() on a NETWORK volume, so `[ -d ]` passed while the listing returned
# "Operation not permitted" - indistinguishable from an empty directory. A
# per-binary probe on that share, same minute, found node held the grant while
# `zsh ls`, `/bin/ls` and `python3` were all DENIED. That share is powered off
# and the destination is now a removable volume, which macOS TCC treats as a
# DIFFERENT category from a network volume - so none of that measurement
# predicts anything about the current destination, in either direction.
#
# WHY node STAYS ANYWAY, on its own merits:
#   - node is already a hard dependency of the 02:00 job, so the constraint
#     costs nothing;
#   - the parser lives here - one field per line, the family regexes, the
#     newline-in-filename safety, the future/zero-size/count logic. Dropping
#     node means rewriting all of it in shell, which is the exact layer where
#     both historical silent false greens lived.
#
# WHAT IS EXPECTED OF TCC NOW, stated so it can be falsified rather than
# assumed: a locally attached volume the user already mounted is not gated the
# way a network volume is, so the 09:00 job should readdir the card without an
# extra grant. That expectation was verified with a throwaway launchd agent
# rather than trusted - see the M16-10 build record for the probe's verbatim
# result. If it ever returns EPERM/EACCES this script reports DENIED, which is
# already an alert, and the remedy is an operator grant - never loosening the
# check.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT ADVERSARIAL REVIEW FOUND IN THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FIX (2026-08-15)
#
# The three-state requirement was met and no condition had been weakened, but
# two SILENT FALSE GREENS survived, and a false green is the one outcome this
# script exists to prevent:
#
#   (a) FUTURE MTIME. The age comparison was `[ "$AGE" -ge "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ]`
#       with no lower bound. An artifact dated in the future yields a NEGATIVE
#       age, which is less than the threshold, so the script logged OK and
#       exited 0 - permanently green. Clock skew on a NAS makes this ordinary,
#       not exotic. A negative age is now its own alert: it means something is
#       wrong with the clock on one side, and "I cannot trust these timestamps"
#       is exactly the state this script must never round down to OK.
#
#   (b) DELIMITER PARSING. The probe emitted `STATE|count|newest|age|err` and
#       the shell split it on "|". A "|" in a filename shifted every field, fed
#       a non-numeric value to `-ge`, and zsh's error left the `if` false - so
#       the script fell through to log OK. Fixed structurally rather than by
#       quoting: the probe now emits ONE FIELD PER LINE.
#
#       Precisely why that is safe, because an earlier version of this comment
#       got it wrong: newlines ARE legal in macOS filenames (tested - the
#       script handles one correctly). The protection is not the filesystem, it
#       is the probe's own matching: the family patterns use `.` and
#       non-multiline anchors, so a name containing a newline cannot match a
#       family in the first place and never reaches the emitted fields. Adding
#       an `s` or `m` flag to those regexes, or switching them to `includes()`,
#       would silently reintroduce field shifting.
#
# Two coverage gaps were also found and closed, both of them cases where the
# check would have stayed green through a real backup failure:
#
#   (c) ONLY THE DB DUMP WAS CHECKED. The nightly job writes three artifact
#       families; the profile tar - which the backup PR itself calls "data that
#       has no other copy" - could have stopped being produced entirely and
#       this check would have reported OK forever. Both families are now
#       checked independently, and either one going stale alerts.
#
#   (d) A ZERO-BYTE ARTIFACT COUNTED AS A BACKUP. A truncated or failed dump
#       that still created its file passed. Size is now asserted.
#
# The check distinguishes these outcomes and alerts on all but the first.
# THE EXIT CODE IS PART OF THE MESSAGE: 3 means the card is not here, 1 means
# the backups are broken, 4 means the second copy on intel is. They are
# different problems with different remedies and a single undifferentiated 1
# is worth exactly what the three jobs sitting at 1 for two days were worth.
#
#   exit 0
#   OK                fresh, non-empty artifact in every family, both legs sound
#   exit 3 - the card, not the backups
#   CARD_ABSENT       the backup card is not mounted at all
#   WRONG_CARD        a volume is mounted there, but not the backup card
#   exit 1 - the SD64 leg
#   STALE             newest artifact in a family older than the threshold
#   FUTURE            newest artifact is dated ahead of now - clocks disagree
#   ZEROSIZE          newest artifact exists but is empty
#   EMPTY             readdir succeeded, a family has no artifacts at all
#   DENIED            readdir refused - CANNOT VERIFY (the state that used to lie)
#   UNREACHABLE       directory absent / not a directory
#   ERROR             anything else, including an unparseable probe
#   exit 4 - the intel leg
#   INTEL_UNREACHABLE intel could not be reached, so it could not be verified
#   INTEL_DEGRADED    kura intel verify reported a real defect there
#   INTEL_STALE       the newest container on intel is too old - the leg has
#                     stopped being fed, which `verify` alone reports as OK
#                     forever and is the precise defect this leg exists to catch
#   INTEL_UNCHECKABLE this check could not determine the intel leg's state at
#                     all (kura absent, or output it could not parse). NOT a
#                     report that intel is fine - the same distinction DENIED
#                     draws for the SD64 leg
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

REPO_ROOT="${0:A:h}/../.."
LOG="$HOME/Library/Logs/careerforge-backup-liveness.log"

envval() { grep -m1 "^$1=" "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-; }

# printf, not echo: zsh's builtin `echo` EXPANDS backslash escapes, and these
# messages embed a filename read off the backup share. A file named
# `careerforge-db-2026\n<a forged OK line>\c.dump.age` injected a whole fake
# entry into this log and suppressed the trailing newline so the next real
# entry fused onto it - forging the very artifact RUNBOOKS tells the operator
# to read. Same attacker capability as the osascript injection below, against
# the evidence instead of the machine.
log()   { printf '%s %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z')" "$1" >> "$LOG"; }

# The message is passed as an ARGUMENT to osascript, never interpolated into
# the AppleScript source.
#
# This was a live command-injection path, found on the third adversarial pass
# and present since the first version of this file. Several alert messages
# embed $NEWEST - a filename read off the backup share - and the family regex
# allows a filename to contain a double quote. Interpolating it closed the
# AppleScript string literal and the rest of the name was evaluated as code.
# Reproduced exactly:
#
#   osascript -e 'set x to "ZERO BYTES (careerforge-db-2026" \
#     & (do shell script "echo INJECTED-AND-EXECUTED") & ".dump.age)"
#     return x'
#   -> ZERO BYTES (careerforge-db-2026INJECTED-AND-EXECUTED.dump.age)
#
# So anyone who could write a file to that SMB share got code execution as
# Carlos at 09:00, from a check whose entire job is to be trustworthy. The
# `on run {m}` form takes the text as data; the same payload now comes back
# as a literal string.
#
# alert_code takes the EXIT CODE first; alert() is the exit-1 shorthand every
# pre-existing call site still uses unchanged. The distinct codes are for a
# human running this by hand and for the plants, which assert on them - they
# are deliberately NOT read back out of `launchctl list`, whose status column
# was measured on 2026-09-02 to be "(never exited)" after a re-bootstrap and is
# therefore not the last exit status at all.
#
# Every alert carries the intel leg's summary, so no failure on one leg can
# hide the state of the other. INTEL_SUMMARY is initialised before the first
# reachable alert for exactly that reason.
INTEL_SUMMARY="intel leg: NOT CHECKED (this run failed before the intel leg was evaluated)"

alert_code() {
  log "ALERT [$2]: $3 | $INTEL_SUMMARY"
  osascript -e 'on run {m}
display notification m with title "CareerForge backup ALERT" sound name "Basso"
end run' -- "$3"
  exit "$1"
}
alert() { alert_code 1 "$1" "$2"; }

# Threshold resolution order: process environment, then .env, then the default.
# RUNBOOKS documented this as configurable while the first version read the
# process environment ONLY - and launchd supplies no environment, so it was
# permanently 26 in production and an operator who set it in .env got silence.
# A key that is PRESENT but empty is an operator typo, not an absence, so it is
# refused rather than silently defaulted - "I set it and nothing happened" is
# the failure this whole file exists to stop.
# An empty value in the PROCESS environment is refused too, not just an empty
# one in .env. The first version of this only covered .env, so
# `BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS= ./check` silently fell back to 26 - the same
# "I set it and nothing happened" failure in the other half of the lookup.
if [ "${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS+set}" = set ] && [ -z "${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS}" ]; then
  alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is set to an empty value in the environment - refusing to guess a threshold"
fi

MAX_AGE_HOURS="${BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS-}"
if [ -z "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ]; then
  if grep -q '^BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null; then
    MAX_AGE_HOURS="$(envval BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS)"
    [ -z "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ] && alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is set to an empty value in .env - refusing to guess a threshold"
  else
    MAX_AGE_HOURS=26
  fi
fi
# Positive match, and >= 1. Zero would alert STALE every single morning on a
# perfectly healthy backup, which is the alert-fatigue outcome this file was
# written to end - reachable from a one-character typo.
# BOUNDED, not just numeric. `^[0-9]+$` alone admits a value zsh arithmetic
# cannot represent: at 19+ digits the comparison below emits "number truncated
# after 18 digits", evaluates FALSE, and control falls through to `log OK` -
# the original defect again, in the one place the value is operator-supplied.
# Six digits is 114 years of hours; nothing legitimate is longer.
[[ "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" =~ '^[0-9]{1,6}$' ]] \
  || alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS ('$MAX_AGE_HOURS') is not a 1-6 digit positive integer - refusing to guess a threshold"
[ "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" -lt 1 ] \
  && alert ERROR "BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS is $MAX_AGE_HOURS - a threshold below 1 hour would alert every morning on a healthy backup"

# BACKUP_DIR resolution: PROCESS ENVIRONMENT first, then .env (M16-10 D24).
#
# The agent's plist supplies BACKUP_DIR in EnvironmentVariables, so this job no
# longer depends on .env for the destination, and a stale .env entry cannot
# point the 09:00 check at a path the 02:00 job is not writing to - the two
# would then disagree about what "the backup dir" means and the check would
# alert on an empty directory while healthy artifacts landed elsewhere. .env
# remains the fallback so an operator running this by hand, in a shell with no
# environment, gets the same answer the agent gets.
#
# A key PRESENT but EMPTY is refused rather than falling through to the next
# source - the same rule BACKUP_LIVENESS_MAX_AGE_HOURS follows above, for the
# same reason: "I set it and nothing happened" is the failure this file exists
# to end. Both sources are checked for it independently.
if [ "${BACKUP_DIR+set}" = set ] && [ -z "${BACKUP_DIR}" ]; then
  alert UNREACHABLE "BACKUP_DIR is set to an empty value in the environment - refusing to guess the backup destination"
fi
if [ -z "${BACKUP_DIR-}" ]; then
  if grep -q '^BACKUP_DIR=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null; then
    BACKUP_DIR="$(envval BACKUP_DIR)"
    [ -z "$BACKUP_DIR" ] && alert UNREACHABLE "BACKUP_DIR is set to an empty value in .env - refusing to guess the backup destination"
  fi
fi
[ -z "$BACKUP_DIR" ] && alert UNREACHABLE "BACKUP_DIR is set in neither the agent's plist (EnvironmentVariables) nor .env - the nightly job cannot be running"

# THE SMB REMOUNT BLOCK THAT USED TO LIVE HERE IS DELETED, NOT DISABLED
# (M16-10 D24). It mirrored the backup wrapper's remount courtesy for the SMB
# share at .97, which Carlos powered off on 2026-09-01. The destination is now
# a removable card: it is mounted or it is absent, and absent is a state this
# check must REPORT (CARD_ABSENT below), never quietly try to repair. Anything
# that reintroduces an `osascript -e "mount volume ..."` here must re-read the
# injection note above first - the deleted block passed the .env value as an
# ARGUMENT precisely because interpolating it was executable.

# BACKUP_CARD_ID: same resolution order and same empty-is-a-typo rule.
if [ "${BACKUP_CARD_ID+set}" = set ] && [ -z "${BACKUP_CARD_ID}" ]; then
  alert ERROR "BACKUP_CARD_ID is set to an empty value in the environment - refusing to guess which volume is the backup card"
fi
if [ -z "${BACKUP_CARD_ID-}" ]; then
  if grep -q '^BACKUP_CARD_ID=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env" 2>/dev/null; then
    BACKUP_CARD_ID="$(envval BACKUP_CARD_ID)"
    [ -z "$BACKUP_CARD_ID" ] && alert ERROR "BACKUP_CARD_ID is set to an empty value in .env - refusing to guess which volume is the backup card"
  fi
fi
BACKUP_CARD_ID="${BACKUP_CARD_ID-}"

# A destination under /Volumes is removable media by definition, and on
# removable media "some volume is mounted at that path" is not "the backup card
# is present" - any card mounts at /Volumes/<its own name>. Without an id there
# is no way to tell them apart, so an unset id on such a destination is refused
# rather than quietly skipping the card checks: a gate that disables itself
# when its config is missing is the silent-green shape this file exists to end.
case "$BACKUP_DIR" in
  /Volumes/*)
    [ -n "$BACKUP_CARD_ID" ] || alert ERROR "BACKUP_DIR ($BACKUP_DIR) is on a removable volume but BACKUP_CARD_ID is not set - this check cannot tell the backup card from any other volume mounted at that path" ;;
esac

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INTEL LEG - evaluated HERE, alerted on at the END (M16-10 D26, D30)
#
# EVALUATED BEFORE the card gate on purpose: if it ran after, a missing card
# would exit first and the operator would fix the card while never learning
# that the second copy had stopped being fed weeks ago. Its summary rides on
# every alert this script raises, so neither leg can hide the other.
#
# INTEGRITY comes from `kura intel verify` and FRESHNESS from
# `kura intel status`, and it takes both. `kura intel verify` has no staleness
# check at all: a leg that stopped being fed passes it forever, so a criterion
# resting on verify alone is satisfiable by a dead leg - which is the precise
# failure this whole story exists to prevent.
#
# THE THRESHOLD IS ONE DAY BECAUSE THE INSTRUMENT EMITS DAYS. `kura intel
# status` prints per-project age as `<N>d`, computed as elapsed seconds floor
# divided by 86400 - not a calendar-day delta - so the boundary sits at exactly
# 24h elapsed. A container written at 21:00 reads 0d at the 09:00 check (about
# 12h old) and one missed 21:00 run reads 1d (about 36h), leaving 12 hours of
# margin on both sides. An hours-based threshold cannot be applied here: 26
# compared against day-granular output is 0 < 26 when healthy AND 1 < 26 when
# stale, a detector that can never fire.
INTEL_MAX_AGE_DAYS=1
INTEL_STATE=OK
INTEL_DETAIL=""

if [ "$#" -eq 0 ]; then
  INTEL_STATE=NOT_CHECKED
  INTEL_DETAIL="no kura project names were given on the command line"
elif ! command -v kura >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  INTEL_STATE=UNCHECKABLE
  INTEL_DETAIL="kura is not on PATH, so the intel leg could not be checked"
else
  INTEL_VERIFY_OUT="$(kura intel verify "$@" 2>&1)"; INTEL_VERIFY_RC=$?
  if printf '%s' "$INTEL_VERIFY_OUT" | grep -q 'not reachable'; then
    INTEL_STATE=UNREACHABLE
    INTEL_DETAIL="kura could not reach the intel host"
  elif [ "$INTEL_VERIFY_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
    INTEL_STATE=DEGRADED
    INTEL_DETAIL="kura intel verify: $(printf '%s' "$INTEL_VERIFY_OUT" | grep -E 'INVARIANT VIOLATION|MISSING SIDECAR|CHECKSUM FAIL|NO ARCHIVES|ORPHAN SIDECAR' | head -3 | tr '\n' ';')"
  else
    INTEL_STATUS_OUT="$(kura intel status "$@" 2>&1)"; INTEL_STATUS_RC=$?
    if [ "$INTEL_STATUS_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
      INTEL_STATE=UNREACHABLE
      INTEL_DETAIL="kura intel status exited $INTEL_STATUS_RC, so the leg could not be aged"
    else
      # One "<name><TAB><days|NEVER>" line per project it could age.
      INTEL_AGES="$(printf '%s\n' "$INTEL_STATUS_OUT" | awk '
        /never backed up/ { print $1 "\tNEVER"; next }
        $2 ~ /^[0-9]+d$/  { s = $2; sub(/d$/, "", s); print $1 "\t" s }
      ')"
      INTEL_AGES_N="$(printf '%s\n' "$INTEL_AGES" | grep -c '.')"
      if [ "$INTEL_AGES_N" -ne "$#" ]; then
        # FAIL CLOSED. If kura's output shape ever changes, an unparsed leg
        # must not read as a healthy one - that is the whole defect class.
        INTEL_STATE=UNCHECKABLE
        INTEL_DETAIL="aged only $INTEL_AGES_N of $# project(s) from kura intel status - refusing to call the intel leg healthy on output this check could not read"
      else
        INTEL_WORST=0; INTEL_WORST_NAME=""; INTEL_NEVER=""
        while IFS=$'\t' read -r _nm _ag; do
          [ -z "$_nm" ] && continue
          if [ "$_ag" = NEVER ]; then INTEL_NEVER="$_nm"; continue; fi
          if [ "$_ag" -gt "$INTEL_WORST" ]; then INTEL_WORST="$_ag"; INTEL_WORST_NAME="$_nm"; fi
        done <<< "$INTEL_AGES"
        if [ -n "$INTEL_NEVER" ]; then
          INTEL_STATE=STALE
          INTEL_DETAIL="'$INTEL_NEVER' has never reached intel"
        elif [ "$INTEL_WORST" -ge "$INTEL_MAX_AGE_DAYS" ]; then
          INTEL_STATE=STALE
          INTEL_DETAIL="the newest container for '$INTEL_WORST_NAME' on intel is ${INTEL_WORST}d old (threshold ${INTEL_MAX_AGE_DAYS}d)"
        else
          INTEL_DETAIL="verified, ${INTEL_WORST}d old at worst across $# project(s)"
        fi
      fi
    fi
  fi
fi
INTEL_SUMMARY="intel leg: $INTEL_STATE ($INTEL_DETAIL)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CARD GATE (M16-10 D3, D35)
#
# Card-absent, wrong-card and backup-directory-missing used to produce one
# identical operator experience. They are three different problems with three
# different remedies, and the sentinel lives at the VOLUME ROOT rather than
# inside BACKUP_DIR precisely so "right card, backup directory gone" stays
# expressible - inside the directory, that condition and "wrong card" collapse
# back into one.
#
# `mount`, not `[ -d ]`: a stale mount point can leave a directory stat-able
# with nothing behind it, which is the same shape as the TCC bug above.
if [ -n "$BACKUP_CARD_ID" ]; then
  CARD_VOLUME="${BACKUP_DIR:h}"
  if ! mount | grep -qF " on $CARD_VOLUME ("; then
    alert_code 3 CARD_ABSENT "the backup card is NOT MOUNTED at $CARD_VOLUME - reinsert it. No backup has run since the newest artifact on that card, and none can until it is back."
  fi
  CARD_SENTINEL="$CARD_VOLUME/.careerforge-card"
  if [ ! -f "$CARD_SENTINEL" ]; then
    alert_code 3 WRONG_CARD "a volume is mounted at $CARD_VOLUME but it carries no card sentinel ($CARD_SENTINEL) - this is not the backup card, so nothing here is last night's backup"
  fi
  # Bounded read, and the ids themselves are never logged: the operator needs
  # to know they disagree, not what either one is.
  if [ "$(head -c 4096 "$CARD_SENTINEL" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r\n\t ')" != "$BACKUP_CARD_ID" ]; then
    alert_code 3 WRONG_CARD "the volume mounted at $CARD_VOLUME is NOT the backup card - its sentinel id does not match BACKUP_CARD_ID"
  fi
fi

# node holds the parser and is already a dependency of the 02:00 job (see
# header). If it is missing we CANNOT VERIFY, and per this script's founding
# principle that is an alert.
command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || alert DENIED "node not found on PATH - cannot verify the backup (this check requires node; see header)"

# ONE FIELD PER LINE, fixed order, "-" for absent. Values only, never contents.
# A newline in a filename cannot shift this parse - not because filenames
# cannot contain one (on macOS they can), but because the family regexes below
# use `.` and non-multiline anchors, so such a name never matches a family and
# never reaches these fields. See the header for the full reasoning.
#
#   1 global state   OK | UNREACHABLE | DENIED | ERROR
#   2 errcode        the errno name, or "-"
#   then per family, in the order declared below:
#     count, ageHours, sizeBytes, newestName   ("-" when count is 0)
PROBE="$(node -e '
const fs = require("fs");
const dir = process.argv[1];
const families = [
  ["db",      /^careerforge-db-.*\.dump\.age$/],
  ["profile", /^careerforge-profile-.*\.tar\.age$/],
];
const out = [];
let entries;
try {
  entries = fs.readdirSync(dir);
} catch (e) {
  const code = e.code || "UNKNOWN";
  const state = (code === "EPERM" || code === "EACCES") ? "DENIED"
              : (code === "ENOENT" || code === "ENOTDIR") ? "UNREACHABLE"
              : "ERROR";
  process.stdout.write([state, code].join("\n") + "\n");
  process.exit(0);
}
out.push("OK", "-");
for (const [, re] of families) {
  const files = entries.filter((f) => re.test(f));
  if (files.length === 0) { out.push("0", "-", "-", "-"); continue; }
  let newest = "", mtime = 0, size = 0;
  for (const f of files) {
    let st;
    try { st = fs.statSync(dir + "/" + f); } catch { continue; }
    if (st.mtimeMs > mtime) { mtime = st.mtimeMs; newest = f; size = st.size; }
  }
  if (!newest) { out.push(String(files.length), "-", "-", "-"); continue; }
  // Truncation toward zero would turn a small negative age into 0 and hide a
  // future timestamp, so floor() is deliberate: it keeps negatives negative.
  const age = Math.floor((Date.now() - mtime) / 3600000);
  out.push(String(files.length), String(age), String(size), newest);
}
process.stdout.write(out.join("\n") + "\n");
' "$BACKUP_DIR" 2>/dev/null)"

[ -z "$PROBE" ] && alert ERROR "the liveness probe produced no output - cannot verify the backup"

# Command substitution has already stripped the probe's trailing newline, so
# this splits to exactly the fields the probe emitted - no empty tail element
# to trim. (An earlier version trimmed one anyway; the arithmetic in that trim
# was itself a `zsh -n` error, which is a fair summary of why dead defensive
# code is not free.)
FIELDS=("${(@f)PROBE}")

GLOBAL="${FIELDS[1]}"
ERRCODE="${FIELDS[2]}"

case "$GLOBAL" in
  UNREACHABLE)
    # ENOTDIR reaches here too: BACKUP_DIR exists but is a file, not a
    # directory. The wording covers both rather than asserting "unmounted".
    alert UNREACHABLE "cannot read the backup dir ($BACKUP_DIR) - missing, unmounted, or not a directory ($ERRCODE). Cannot verify last night's backup" ;;
  DENIED)
    alert DENIED "CANNOT VERIFY - readdir refused on the backup dir ($ERRCODE). The backup itself may be fine; this check has lost its access. See the header of scripts/launchd/careerforge-backup-liveness." ;;
  ERROR)
    alert ERROR "unexpected error reading the backup dir ($ERRCODE) - cannot verify" ;;
  OK)
    : ;;
  *)
    alert ERROR "unrecognized probe state '$GLOBAL' - cannot verify" ;;
esac

# Four fields per family, starting after the two global fields.
[ "${#FIELDS[@]}" -ne 10 ] && alert ERROR "the liveness probe returned ${#FIELDS[@]} fields, expected 10 - cannot verify"

FAMILY_NAMES=("database dump" "profile archive")
SUMMARY=""
i=3
for name in "${FAMILY_NAMES[@]}"; do
  COUNT="${FIELDS[$i]}"
  AGE="${FIELDS[$((i + 1))]}"
  SIZE="${FIELDS[$((i + 2))]}"
  NEWEST="${FIELDS[$((i + 3))]}"
  i=$((i + 4))

  [ "$COUNT" = "0" ] && alert EMPTY "NO $name artifacts found in the backup dir at all"

  # Guard every numeric BEFORE comparing. An unguarded comparison against a
  # non-numeric value is how (b) above turned into a logged OK.
  #
  # MATCH POSITIVELY. The first version of this guard excluded characters
  # instead - `*[!0-9-]*` - which accepts any string of digits and hyphens.
  # Adversarial review demonstrated the leak: `--`, `1-2`, `-1-1` and `3-4-5`
  # all pass, both comparisons below then error under zsh and evaluate false,
  # and control falls through to `log OK` - reproducing defect (b) inside the
  # guard written to prevent it. Not reachable through the current probe, which
  # emits only `-?\d+`, NaN or Infinity, but a guard whose own escape hatch
  # fails OPEN is the wrong shape for this file.
  [[ "$AGE" =~ '^-?[0-9]+$' ]] \
    || alert ERROR "could not read the age of the newest $name ('$AGE') - cannot verify"
  [[ "$SIZE" =~ '^[0-9]+$' ]] \
    || alert ERROR "could not read the size of the newest $name ('$SIZE') - cannot verify"

  [ "$AGE" -lt 0 ] && alert FUTURE "the newest $name is dated ${AGE#-}h in the FUTURE ($NEWEST) - the clock on this Mac and the one on the backup share disagree, so no age check here can be trusted"
  [ "$SIZE" -eq 0 ] && alert ZEROSIZE "the newest $name is ZERO BYTES ($NEWEST) - the job created the file but wrote nothing to it"
  [ "$AGE" -ge "$MAX_AGE_HOURS" ] && alert STALE "the newest $name is ${AGE}h old (threshold ${MAX_AGE_HOURS}h) - the nightly job has stopped succeeding. Check ~/Library/Logs/careerforge-backup.log"

  # The newest filename stays in the OK line on purpose. It is the only field
  # that would visibly garble if the probe parse ever shifted again, so an
  # operator reading the log can see the parse is intact, and the plant for
  # the delimiter case has something to assert on beyond the exit code.
  SUMMARY="${SUMMARY}${SUMMARY:+; }${name} ${AGE}h old, ${COUNT} present (${NEWEST})"
done

# The SD64 leg is sound. Now report the intel leg, whose verdict was reached
# before any of the above could exit.
case "$INTEL_STATE" in
  OK|NOT_CHECKED) : ;;
  UNREACHABLE)
    alert_code 4 INTEL_UNREACHABLE "the SECOND copy could not be verified - $INTEL_DETAIL. The SD64 leg is healthy ($SUMMARY), so this is the intel leg alone: check that the machine is awake and on the network." ;;
  DEGRADED)
    alert_code 4 INTEL_DEGRADED "the SECOND copy on intel is DAMAGED - $INTEL_DETAIL. The SD64 leg is healthy ($SUMMARY)." ;;
  STALE)
    alert_code 4 INTEL_STALE "the SECOND copy on intel has STOPPED BEING FED - $INTEL_DETAIL. The SD64 leg is healthy ($SUMMARY), so the archives exist here and are not reaching intel: check kura's 21:00 run." ;;
  UNCHECKABLE)
    alert_code 4 INTEL_UNCHECKABLE "CANNOT VERIFY the second copy on intel - $INTEL_DETAIL. The SD64 leg is healthy ($SUMMARY). This is not a report that intel is fine; it is a report that this check could not find out." ;;
  *)
    alert_code 4 INTEL_UNCHECKABLE "unrecognized intel state '$INTEL_STATE' - cannot verify the second copy" ;;
esac

log "OK: $SUMMARY | $INTEL_SUMMARY"
exit 0
